%% ---- The reporting pass (llms.txt §1.1.7) ----------------------------------
%%
%% What the derived pack already gives this model without being asked: a
%% register per entity, a breakdown per %%enum-bound column, created-per-month,
%% a lifecycle report over both state machines in the diagram's own order, and
%% children-per-parent for all 10 relationships. None of that needed anyone who
%% understands how a studio fills a room.
%%
%% These do. Each of the four roles on the %%rbac matrix above is walked through
%% the four questions — what they open first, what tells them something is wrong
%% today, what they are asked for weekly, and what rots silently if nobody
%% looks — so each can do its whole job from what it can see.

%% -- member: their own classes and credits -----------------------------------
    %%report member-upcoming-bookings title: Places held for upcoming classes entity: Booking help: The screen a member opens to check what they have committed to this week. A held place is a seat nobody else can take, so a member who has forgotten one costs the studio the room. sql: SELECT m.full_name AS member, o.name AS class, i.full_name AS instructor, s.starts_at FROM bus_booking b JOIN bus_member m ON m.id = b.member_id JOIN bus_class_session s ON s.id = b.class_session_id JOIN bus_class_offering o ON o.id = s.class_offering_id LEFT JOIN bus_instructor i ON i.id = s.instructor_id WHERE b.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.deleted_at IS NULL AND b.status = 'held' AND s.starts_at > now() ORDER BY s.starts_at
    %%report member-classes-with-places title: Upcoming classes that still have room entity: ClassSession help: What a member actually wants: not a list of classes, but the ones they can still get into. Capacity minus places already held, so a full class never appears. sql: SELECT o.name AS class, o.level, i.full_name AS instructor, s.starts_at, s.capacity - COUNT(b.id) AS places_left FROM bus_class_session s JOIN bus_class_offering o ON o.id = s.class_offering_id LEFT JOIN bus_instructor i ON i.id = s.instructor_id LEFT JOIN bus_booking b ON b.class_session_id = s.id AND b.deleted_at IS NULL AND b.status = 'held' WHERE s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.status = 'scheduled' AND s.starts_at > now() GROUP BY s.id, o.name, o.level, i.full_name, s.starts_at, s.capacity HAVING s.capacity - COUNT(b.id) > 0 ORDER BY s.starts_at
    %%report member-credits-expiring title: Credits about to expire unused entity: ClassPack help: A pack expires on a date nobody is told about, and the credits simply stop existing. Thirty days is enough notice to book them and not so much that the warning is ignored. sql: SELECT m.full_name AS member, p.credits_remaining, p.expires_on, (p.expires_on - now()::date) AS days_left FROM bus_class_pack p JOIN bus_member m ON m.id = p.member_id WHERE p.deleted_at IS NULL AND p.status = 'active' AND p.credits_remaining > 0 AND p.expires_on BETWEEN now()::date AND (now() + interval '30 days')::date ORDER BY p.expires_on
    %%report member-waitlist-standing title: Where each member sits on a waitlist entity: WaitlistEntry help: A member on a waitlist wants one number: how many people are ahead of them. Without it they book a second class they do not need, and then cancel one of the two late. sql: SELECT m.full_name AS member, o.name AS class, s.starts_at, w.queue_position FROM bus_waitlist_entry w JOIN bus_member m ON m.id = w.member_id JOIN bus_class_session s ON s.id = w.class_session_id JOIN bus_class_offering o ON o.id = s.class_offering_id WHERE w.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.deleted_at IS NULL AND w.status = 'waiting' AND s.starts_at > now() ORDER BY s.starts_at, w.queue_position

%% -- receptionist: the desk, today -------------------------------------------
    %%report reception-today-roster title: Today's classes and who is coming entity: ClassSession help: The first thing opened at the desk each morning, and the sheet the register is taken from. Held rather than attended, because attendance is what the day is about to record. sql: SELECT o.name AS class, i.full_name AS instructor, r.name AS room, s.starts_at, s.capacity, COUNT(b.id) AS booked FROM bus_class_session s JOIN bus_class_offering o ON o.id = s.class_offering_id LEFT JOIN bus_instructor i ON i.id = s.instructor_id LEFT JOIN bus_room r ON r.id = s.room_id LEFT JOIN bus_booking b ON b.class_session_id = s.id AND b.deleted_at IS NULL AND b.status = 'held' WHERE s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.status IN ('scheduled', 'running') AND s.starts_at >= date_trunc('day', now()) AND s.starts_at < date_trunc('day', now()) + interval '1 day' GROUP BY s.id, o.name, i.full_name, r.name, s.starts_at, s.capacity ORDER BY s.starts_at
    %%report reception-waitlist-with-space title: People waiting for a class that has room entity: WaitlistEntry help: The exception that should never exist. Somebody is waiting on a session with an empty place, which means the promotion saga did not run or a cancellation freed a seat nobody handed on. Fix it by hand today. sql: SELECT m.full_name AS member, o.name AS class, s.starts_at, w.queue_position, s.capacity - COUNT(b.id) AS places_free FROM bus_waitlist_entry w JOIN bus_member m ON m.id = w.member_id JOIN bus_class_session s ON s.id = w.class_session_id JOIN bus_class_offering o ON o.id = s.class_offering_id LEFT JOIN bus_booking b ON b.class_session_id = s.id AND b.deleted_at IS NULL AND b.status = 'held' WHERE w.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.deleted_at IS NULL AND w.status = 'waiting' AND s.status = 'scheduled' AND s.starts_at > now() GROUP BY w.id, m.full_name, o.name, s.starts_at, w.queue_position, s.capacity HAVING s.capacity - COUNT(b.id) > 0 ORDER BY s.starts_at, w.queue_position
    %%report reception-packs-without-payment title: Class packs with no payment recorded entity: ClassPack help: Credits were issued and no money was ever attached to them. Almost always a card that failed after the pack was created, and it is invisible from the member screen. sql: SELECT m.full_name AS member, p.credits_purchased, p.purchased_on, p.status FROM bus_class_pack p JOIN bus_member m ON m.id = p.member_id LEFT JOIN bus_payment y ON y.class_pack_id = p.id AND y.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE p.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY p.id, m.full_name, p.credits_purchased, p.purchased_on, p.status HAVING COUNT(y.id) = 0 ORDER BY p.purchased_on DESC
    %%report reception-lapsed-holding-credits title: Lapsed members who still hold credits entity: Member help: Someone stopped coming while still owning classes they paid for. It is the cheapest call the desk can make, and the member is usually glad to be reminded. sql: SELECT m.full_name AS member, m.email, m.phone, SUM(p.credits_remaining) AS credits, MIN(p.expires_on) AS first_expiry FROM bus_member m JOIN bus_class_pack p ON p.member_id = m.id AND p.deleted_at IS NULL AND p.status = 'active' AND p.credits_remaining > 0 WHERE m.deleted_at IS NULL AND m.status = 'lapsed' GROUP BY m.id, m.full_name, m.email, m.phone ORDER BY credits DESC
    %%report reception-recent-no-shows title: No-shows worth a phone call entity: Booking help: A no-show costs the studio a seat and usually costs the member a credit. Called the same week it is a courtesy; called a month later it is an argument about a refund. sql: SELECT m.full_name AS member, m.phone, o.name AS class, s.starts_at, b.fee_waived FROM bus_booking b JOIN bus_member m ON m.id = b.member_id JOIN bus_class_session s ON s.id = b.class_session_id JOIN bus_class_offering o ON o.id = s.class_offering_id WHERE b.deleted_at IS NULL AND b.status = 'no_show' AND s.starts_at > now() - interval '14 days' ORDER BY s.starts_at DESC

%% -- instructor: the room they are about to walk into ------------------------
    %%report instructor-upcoming-roster title: Each instructor's next classes and headcount entity: ClassSession help: What an instructor checks the night before. Numbers change how a class is taught — four people is a workshop, twenty is a routine — and the room assignment is how they know where to go. sql: SELECT i.full_name AS instructor, o.name AS class, o.level, r.name AS room, s.starts_at, COUNT(b.id) AS booked, s.capacity FROM bus_class_session s JOIN bus_instructor i ON i.id = s.instructor_id JOIN bus_class_offering o ON o.id = s.class_offering_id LEFT JOIN bus_room r ON r.id = s.room_id LEFT JOIN bus_booking b ON b.class_session_id = s.id AND b.deleted_at IS NULL AND b.status = 'held' WHERE s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.status = 'scheduled' AND s.starts_at BETWEEN now() AND now() + interval '7 days' GROUP BY s.id, i.full_name, o.name, o.level, r.name, s.starts_at, s.capacity ORDER BY s.starts_at
    %%report instructor-under-filled-sessions title: Classes too empty to run entity: ClassSession help: A class within three days that has almost nobody in it is a decision — cancel it now and free the instructor, or promote it. Left alone it runs at a loss and the instructor finds out on arrival. sql: SELECT o.name AS class, i.full_name AS instructor, s.starts_at, COUNT(b.id) AS booked, s.capacity FROM bus_class_session s JOIN bus_class_offering o ON o.id = s.class_offering_id LEFT JOIN bus_instructor i ON i.id = s.instructor_id LEFT JOIN bus_booking b ON b.class_session_id = s.id AND b.deleted_at IS NULL AND b.status = 'held' WHERE s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.status = 'scheduled' AND s.starts_at BETWEEN now() AND now() + interval '3 days' GROUP BY s.id, o.name, i.full_name, s.starts_at, s.capacity HAVING COUNT(b.id) < 3 ORDER BY s.starts_at
    %%report instructor-attendance-rate title: Attendance against places held, by instructor entity: Instructor chart: bar x: instructor y: attendance_pct help: How many of the people who booked actually came. A consistently low rate is not the instructor's fault, but it is their signal — a class scheduled at the wrong hour, or one people book to keep a habit and skip. sql: SELECT i.full_name AS instructor, COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE b.status = 'attended') AS attended, COUNT(*) AS places, ROUND(100.0 * COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE b.status = 'attended') / NULLIF(COUNT(*), 0)) AS attendance_pct FROM bus_booking b JOIN bus_class_session s ON s.id = b.class_session_id JOIN bus_instructor i ON i.id = s.instructor_id WHERE b.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.status = 'completed' AND b.status IN ('attended', 'no_show', 'forfeited') GROUP BY i.id, i.full_name ORDER BY attendance_pct

%% -- manager: whether the studio is working ----------------------------------
    %%report manager-revenue-by-month title: Money taken by month entity: Payment chart: line x: month y: revenue help: The one number the owner asks for, and the only report here that a bank would recognise. Taken from payments received rather than packs sold, so an unpaid pack never counts as income. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', y.received_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, SUM(y.amount) AS revenue, COUNT(*) AS payments FROM bus_payment y WHERE y.deleted_at IS NULL AND y.received_at > now() - interval '12 months' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report manager-room-utilisation title: How full each room actually runs entity: Room chart: bar x: room y: fill_pct help: Places sold against places built, per room. A room consistently under half full is either the wrong size for what is scheduled in it or a slot nobody wants, and both are fixable from the timetable. sql: SELECT r.name AS room, r.capacity AS seats, COUNT(b.id) AS booked, ROUND(100.0 * COUNT(b.id) / NULLIF(SUM(s.capacity), 0)) AS fill_pct FROM bus_class_session s JOIN bus_room r ON r.id = s.room_id LEFT JOIN bus_booking b ON b.class_session_id = s.id AND b.deleted_at IS NULL AND b.status IN ('held', 'attended') WHERE s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.status IN ('scheduled', 'completed') AND s.starts_at > now() - interval '90 days' GROUP BY r.id, r.name, r.capacity ORDER BY fill_pct
    %%report manager-cancelled-sessions title: Classes the studio cancelled, and why entity: ClassSession help: Every cancelled session is members told no. The reason column is the point: a handful of instructor illnesses is life, a pattern of under-booking is a timetable that needs changing. sql: SELECT o.name AS class, i.full_name AS instructor, s.starts_at, s.cancellation_reason FROM bus_class_session s JOIN bus_class_offering o ON o.id = s.class_offering_id LEFT JOIN bus_instructor i ON i.id = s.instructor_id WHERE s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.status = 'cancelled' AND s.starts_at > now() - interval '90 days' ORDER BY s.starts_at DESC
    %%report manager-offering-demand title: Which classes people actually book entity: ClassOffering chart: bar x: class y: booked help: What to put more of on the timetable and what to retire. Bookings per offering over a quarter, which is long enough that one bad week does not decide it. sql: SELECT o.name AS class, o.level, COUNT(b.id) AS booked, COUNT(DISTINCT s.id) AS sessions FROM bus_class_offering o LEFT JOIN bus_class_session s ON s.class_offering_id = o.id AND s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.starts_at > now() - interval '90 days' LEFT JOIN bus_booking b ON b.class_session_id = s.id AND b.deleted_at IS NULL AND b.status IN ('held', 'attended') WHERE o.deleted_at IS NULL AND o.is_active GROUP BY o.id, o.name, o.level ORDER BY booked DESC
    %%report manager-members-drifting title: Active members who have stopped coming entity: Member help: The report that rots silently. A member still marked active who has not attended in sixty days has already left; nobody has told the system and nobody has phoned them. This is the list churn is prevented from. sql: SELECT m.full_name AS member, m.email, m.joined_on, MAX(s.starts_at) AS last_attended FROM bus_member m LEFT JOIN bus_booking b ON b.member_id = m.id AND b.deleted_at IS NULL AND b.status = 'attended' LEFT JOIN bus_class_session s ON s.id = b.class_session_id AND s.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE m.deleted_at IS NULL AND m.status = 'active' GROUP BY m.id, m.full_name, m.email, m.joined_on HAVING MAX(s.starts_at) IS NULL OR MAX(s.starts_at) < now() - interval '60 days' ORDER BY last_attended NULLS FIRST
    %%report manager-waived-fees title: Cancellation fees written off entity: Booking help: Every waiver is money the studio chose not to take, and the reason is free text because the judgement is human. Read together they show whether the cutoff rule is being applied or quietly ignored. sql: SELECT m.full_name AS member, o.name AS class, s.starts_at, b.waiver_reason FROM bus_booking b JOIN bus_member m ON m.id = b.member_id JOIN bus_class_session s ON s.id = b.class_session_id JOIN bus_class_offering o ON o.id = s.class_offering_id WHERE b.deleted_at IS NULL AND b.fee_waived AND b.cancelled_at > now() - interval '90 days' ORDER BY b.cancelled_at DESC


%% -- the whole studio: questions every role can ask of the eight tables it shares ----
%% Payment is the one table members and instructors cannot read, so everything below
%% except the payment reports can be run by all four roles, and counts for each of them.
    %%report members-by-status title: Members by status entity: Member chart: pie x: status y: members help: Who the studio can still sell to. Active members may book, lapsed ones are the re-engagement list, suspended ones are barred. The proportions say whether the base is growing or quietly emptying. sql: SELECT status, COUNT(*) AS members FROM bus_member WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report members-joined-per-month title: New members per month entity: Member chart: line x: month y: joined help: Sign-ups are the top of the funnel. A flat or falling line six months before a new term is the early warning to start marketing, not the term itself. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', joined_on), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS joined FROM bus_member WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report members-marketing-opt-in-share title: How many members agreed to announcements entity: Member chart: pie x: consent y: members help: Announcements can only go to members who asked for them. The size of the opted-in group is the real reach of any campaign, whatever the size of the member list. sql: SELECT CASE WHEN marketing_opt_in THEN 'opted in' ELSE 'not opted in' END AS consent, COUNT(*) AS members FROM bus_member WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1
    %%report members-tenure-bands title: How long members have been with the studio entity: Member chart: bar x: tenure y: members help: Loyalty in four bands. A studio full of members under three months old is growing fast and leaking fast; one with a heavy over-two-years band has a community worth protecting. sql: SELECT CASE WHEN joined_on > now()::date - 90 THEN '0-3 months' WHEN joined_on > now()::date - 365 THEN '3-12 months' WHEN joined_on > now()::date - 730 THEN '1-2 years' ELSE 'over 2 years' END AS tenure, COUNT(*) AS members FROM bus_member WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY MIN(joined_on) DESC
    %%report members-never-booked title: Members who have never booked a class entity: Member chart: bar x: status y: members help: Signed up, never came. They cost nothing to keep and nothing is earned from them, and the first booking is the cheapest conversion the studio will ever get. Split by status to show which are still reachable. sql: SELECT m.status, COUNT(*) AS members FROM bus_member m WHERE m.deleted_at IS NULL AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM bus_booking b WHERE b.member_id = m.id AND b.deleted_at IS NULL) GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report members-bookings-distribution title: How many bookings members make entity: Member chart: bar x: bookings_made y: members help: A few members book constantly and most book rarely. The shape of this curve tells the studio whether it runs on regulars or on drop-ins, and which one a price change would hurt. sql: SELECT n AS bookings_made, COUNT(*) AS members FROM (SELECT m.id, COUNT(b.id) AS n FROM bus_member m LEFT JOIN bus_booking b ON b.member_id = m.id AND b.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE m.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY m.id) t GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report members-packs-bought-distribution title: How many packs members have bought entity: Member chart: bar x: packs_bought y: members help: One pack is a trial; three is a habit. Members by number of packs bought is the simplest honest measure of repeat purchase. sql: SELECT n AS packs_bought, COUNT(*) AS members FROM (SELECT m.id, COUNT(p.id) AS n FROM bus_member m LEFT JOIN bus_class_pack p ON p.member_id = m.id AND p.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE m.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY m.id) t GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report members-active-without-usable-pack title: Active members with no usable pack, by joining year entity: Member chart: bar x: joined_year y: members help: An active member who holds no unexpired credits cannot book, and will not tell anyone why. This is the list to offer a pack to, grouped by when they joined so old and new members can be approached differently. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('year', m.joined_on), 'YYYY') AS joined_year, COUNT(*) AS members FROM bus_member m WHERE m.deleted_at IS NULL AND m.status = 'active' AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM bus_class_pack p WHERE p.member_id = m.id AND p.deleted_at IS NULL AND p.status = 'active' AND p.expires_on >= now()::date) GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report members-contactable-by-phone title: Members the desk can phone entity: Member chart: pie x: reachable y: members help: Same-day cancellations are phoned, not emailed. A member with no number on file cannot be reached when it matters, and the share without one is how big that gap is. sql: SELECT CASE WHEN phone IS NULL OR phone = '' THEN 'no phone on file' ELSE 'phone on file' END AS reachable, COUNT(*) AS members FROM bus_member WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1
    %%report instructors-active-share title: Instructors who can still be scheduled entity: Instructor chart: pie x: state y: instructors help: Inactive instructors keep their history and cannot take new sessions. The split shows how deep the bench is before a holiday rota is written. sql: SELECT CASE WHEN is_active THEN 'active' ELSE 'inactive' END AS state, COUNT(*) AS instructors FROM bus_instructor WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1
    %%report instructors-sessions-taught title: Sessions taught per instructor entity: Instructor chart: bar x: instructor y: sessions help: Teaching load, side by side. An instructor teaching three times what a colleague does is either the studio's favourite or about to burn out. sql: SELECT i.full_name AS instructor, COUNT(s.id) AS sessions FROM bus_instructor i LEFT JOIN bus_class_session s ON s.instructor_id = i.id AND s.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE i.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report instructors-fill-rate title: How full each instructor's classes run entity: Instructor chart: bar x: instructor y: fill_pct help: Places taken against places offered, per instructor. A high rate is demand for the person, not only the class, and it is the number that justifies a better slot. sql: SELECT i.full_name AS instructor, ROUND(AVG(100.0 * f.taken / NULLIF(f.capacity, 0)), 1) AS fill_pct FROM (SELECT s.id, s.starts_at, s.capacity, s.class_offering_id, s.room_id, s.instructor_id, s.status, COUNT(b.id) FILTER (WHERE b.status <> 'cancelled') AS taken FROM bus_class_session s LEFT JOIN bus_booking b ON b.class_session_id = s.id AND b.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE s.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY s.id) f JOIN bus_instructor i ON i.id = f.instructor_id AND i.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE f.status <> 'cancelled' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report instructors-cancelled-sessions title: Cancelled sessions per instructor entity: Instructor chart: bar x: instructor y: cancelled help: Cancellations by teacher. A cluster around one name is a reliability conversation; an even spread is just illness and the cost of running a studio. sql: SELECT i.full_name AS instructor, COUNT(*) AS cancelled FROM bus_class_session s JOIN bus_instructor i ON i.id = s.instructor_id AND i.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.status = 'cancelled' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report instructors-no-show-rate title: No-show share of bookings by instructor entity: Instructor chart: bar x: instructor y: no_show_pct help: The share of people who booked a place and did not come. High for one instructor and low for the rest says something about the class slot, not about the people. sql: SELECT i.full_name AS instructor, ROUND(100.0 * COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE b.status = 'no_show') / NULLIF(COUNT(*), 0), 1) AS no_show_pct FROM bus_booking b JOIN bus_class_session s ON s.id = b.class_session_id AND s.deleted_at IS NULL JOIN bus_instructor i ON i.id = s.instructor_id AND i.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE b.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report instructors-class-variety title: How many different classes each instructor teaches entity: Instructor chart: bar x: instructor y: classes help: An instructor who can cover four classes can cover four absences. Variety is the studio's resilience, measured per person. sql: SELECT i.full_name AS instructor, COUNT(DISTINCT s.class_offering_id) AS classes FROM bus_instructor i LEFT JOIN bus_class_session s ON s.instructor_id = i.id AND s.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE i.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report instructors-hours-taught-per-month title: Teaching hours per month entity: Instructor chart: line x: month y: hours help: Hours actually put on the timetable, month by month, from completed sessions. The line to read against the payroll for teaching staff. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', s.starts_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, ROUND(SUM(EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM (s.ends_at - s.starts_at)) / 3600)::numeric, 1) AS hours FROM bus_class_session s WHERE s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.status = 'completed' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report rooms-by-capacity title: Rooms by size entity: Room chart: bar x: room y: seats help: What each space can hold. The ceiling every session's capacity is set under, and the first thing to check before promising a class to a waiting list. sql: SELECT name AS room, capacity AS seats FROM bus_room WHERE deleted_at IS NULL ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report rooms-sessions-hosted title: Sessions hosted per room entity: Room chart: bar x: room y: sessions help: How the timetable is spread across the building. One room carrying most of the sessions is a bottleneck waiting for a clash. sql: SELECT r.name AS room, COUNT(s.id) AS sessions FROM bus_room r LEFT JOIN bus_class_session s ON s.room_id = r.id AND s.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE r.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report rooms-idle-days title: Days since each active room last held a class entity: Room chart: bar x: room y: days_idle help: An active room nobody has taught in for a month is rent without revenue. Rooms never used show the longest bar. sql: SELECT r.name AS room, COALESCE(now()::date - MAX(s.starts_at)::date, 999) AS days_idle FROM bus_room r LEFT JOIN bus_class_session s ON s.room_id = r.id AND s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.status <> 'cancelled' AND s.starts_at < now() WHERE r.deleted_at IS NULL AND r.is_active GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report rooms-seats-given-away title: Seats a room has that its sessions never offer entity: Room chart: bar x: room y: unused_seats help: A session's capacity is seeded from its room and often lowered. The gap, averaged per room, is floor space the studio built and chooses not to sell. sql: SELECT r.name AS room, ROUND(AVG(r.capacity - s.capacity), 1) AS unused_seats FROM bus_class_session s JOIN bus_room r ON r.id = s.room_id AND r.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE s.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report offerings-by-level title: Classes on offer by level entity: ClassOffering chart: pie x: level y: offerings help: The shape of the timetable's ambition. Too many beginner classes and nobody progresses; too few and nobody starts. sql: SELECT level, COUNT(*) AS offerings FROM bus_class_offering WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND is_active GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report offerings-credit-cost-mix title: Classes by credit cost entity: ClassOffering chart: bar x: credits y: offerings help: What a place costs, in credits. A timetable where everything costs one credit has no premium tier, and one where everything costs three prices out the people who are still deciding. sql: SELECT credit_cost AS credits, COUNT(*) AS offerings FROM bus_class_offering WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND is_active GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report offerings-duration-mix title: Classes by length entity: ClassOffering chart: bar x: minutes y: offerings help: How long the studio's classes run. Duration decides how many sessions fit in a room in a day, so it is a capacity decision disguised as a catalogue field. sql: SELECT duration_minutes AS minutes, COUNT(*) AS offerings FROM bus_class_offering WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND is_active GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report offerings-sessions-scheduled title: Sessions scheduled per class entity: ClassOffering chart: bar x: class y: sessions help: How often each class actually runs. A popular class run once a fortnight is a missed opportunity; an unpopular one run daily is a mistake. sql: SELECT o.name AS class, COUNT(s.id) AS sessions FROM bus_class_offering o LEFT JOIN bus_class_session s ON s.class_offering_id = o.id AND s.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE o.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report offerings-average-attendance title: Average people through the door per class entity: ClassOffering chart: bar x: class y: attended help: Attendance, not bookings: who actually turned up. The gap between this and the bookings figure is the cost of no-shows and late cancellations. sql: SELECT o.name AS class, ROUND(AVG(a.attended), 1) AS attended FROM (SELECT s.class_offering_id, COUNT(b.id) FILTER (WHERE b.status = 'attended') AS attended FROM bus_class_session s LEFT JOIN bus_booking b ON b.class_session_id = s.id AND b.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.status = 'completed' GROUP BY s.id) a JOIN bus_class_offering o ON o.id = a.class_offering_id AND o.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report offerings-cancellation-rate title: Share of each class's sessions the studio cancelled entity: ClassOffering chart: bar x: class y: cancelled_pct help: A class that is cancelled often is being scheduled where nobody wants it. The percentage, not the count, so a busy class is not punished for its volume. sql: SELECT o.name AS class, ROUND(100.0 * COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE s.status = 'cancelled') / NULLIF(COUNT(*), 0), 1) AS cancelled_pct FROM bus_class_session s JOIN bus_class_offering o ON o.id = s.class_offering_id AND o.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE s.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report offerings-retired-still-scheduled title: Retired classes that still have sessions ahead entity: ClassOffering chart: bar x: class y: upcoming help: A class marked inactive should have no future sessions. Any that do are a timetable that disagrees with the catalogue, and a member can still book them. sql: SELECT o.name AS class, COUNT(s.id) AS upcoming FROM bus_class_offering o JOIN bus_class_session s ON s.class_offering_id = o.id AND s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.starts_at > now() AND s.status = 'scheduled' WHERE o.deleted_at IS NULL AND NOT o.is_active GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report sessions-by-status title: Sessions by status entity: ClassSession chart: pie x: status y: sessions help: Where the timetable stands: how much is still to come, how much ran, how much was cancelled. The cancelled slice is the one to keep small. sql: SELECT status, COUNT(*) AS sessions FROM bus_class_session WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report sessions-per-month title: Sessions scheduled per month entity: ClassSession chart: line x: month y: sessions help: The volume of teaching the studio puts on, month by month. Read against the sign-up line to see whether supply is keeping up with demand. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', starts_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS sessions FROM bus_class_session WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report sessions-by-weekday title: Sessions by day of the week entity: ClassSession chart: bar x: weekday y: sessions help: How the week is loaded. A studio that teaches heavily on Tuesday and barely on Sunday has made that choice, deliberately or not. sql: SELECT to_char(starts_at, 'Dy') AS weekday, COUNT(*) AS sessions FROM bus_class_session WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND status <> 'cancelled' GROUP BY 1, EXTRACT(ISODOW FROM starts_at) ORDER BY EXTRACT(ISODOW FROM starts_at)
    %%report sessions-by-start-hour title: Sessions by start hour entity: ClassSession chart: bar x: hour y: sessions help: When classes begin. Clusters at 18:00 and 19:00 are the after-work peak, and a gap in the middle of the day is either a lull or an opportunity. sql: SELECT EXTRACT(HOUR FROM starts_at)::int AS hour, COUNT(*) AS sessions FROM bus_class_session WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND status <> 'cancelled' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report sessions-fill-by-weekday title: How full classes are, by day of the week entity: ClassSession chart: bar x: weekday y: fill_pct help: Demand by day, not supply by day. The days with the highest fill are where another session pays for itself; the lowest are candidates for a swap. sql: SELECT to_char(f.starts_at, 'Dy') AS weekday, ROUND(AVG(100.0 * f.taken / NULLIF(f.capacity, 0)), 1) AS fill_pct FROM (SELECT s.id, s.starts_at, s.capacity, s.class_offering_id, s.room_id, s.instructor_id, s.status, COUNT(b.id) FILTER (WHERE b.status <> 'cancelled') AS taken FROM bus_class_session s LEFT JOIN bus_booking b ON b.class_session_id = s.id AND b.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE s.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY s.id) f WHERE f.status <> 'cancelled' GROUP BY 1, EXTRACT(ISODOW FROM f.starts_at) ORDER BY EXTRACT(ISODOW FROM f.starts_at)
    %%report sessions-cancellation-reasons title: Why the studio cancels classes entity: ClassSession chart: bar x: reason y: sessions help: The stated reasons, counted. A reason that repeats is a process problem with a name, and one that is blank is a cancellation nobody had to justify. sql: SELECT COALESCE(NULLIF(TRIM(cancellation_reason), ''), 'no reason given') AS reason, COUNT(*) AS sessions FROM bus_class_session WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND status = 'cancelled' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 15
    %%report sessions-cancelled-per-month title: Cancelled sessions per month entity: ClassSession chart: line x: month y: cancelled help: Whether cancellations are getting rarer or more common. A spike after a timetable change is the change's fault. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', cancelled_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS cancelled FROM bus_class_session WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND status = 'cancelled' AND cancelled_at IS NOT NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report sessions-cancellation-notice title: How much notice members get when a class is cancelled entity: ClassSession chart: bar x: notice y: sessions help: Hours between the cancellation and the class that would have run. Under twelve hours is the same short notice the studio charges members for, and it is worth knowing how often the studio does it to them. sql: SELECT CASE WHEN EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM (starts_at - cancelled_at)) / 3600 < 12 THEN 'under 12 hours' WHEN EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM (starts_at - cancelled_at)) / 3600 < 48 THEN '12-48 hours' ELSE 'over 48 hours' END AS notice, COUNT(*) AS sessions FROM bus_class_session WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND status = 'cancelled' AND cancelled_at IS NOT NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report sessions-upcoming-14-days title: Sessions in the next fortnight, by day entity: ClassSession chart: bar x: day y: sessions help: The near-term timetable at a glance. A day with nothing on it is either a closure or a mistake, and the fortnight is short enough to fix either. sql: SELECT to_char(starts_at, 'MM-DD') AS day, COUNT(*) AS sessions FROM bus_class_session WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND status = 'scheduled' AND starts_at >= now() AND starts_at < now() + interval '14 days' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report sessions-completed-without-attendance title: Completed classes where nobody was marked present entity: ClassSession chart: bar x: month y: sessions help: A session that ran with no attendance recorded either had an empty room or a register nobody filled in. Both are worth knowing, and the second silently falsifies every attendance figure. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', s.starts_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS sessions FROM bus_class_session s WHERE s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.status = 'completed' AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM bus_booking b WHERE b.class_session_id = s.id AND b.deleted_at IS NULL AND b.status = 'attended') GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report sessions-late-starts title: Minutes classes start late, by instructor entity: ClassSession chart: bar x: instructor y: avg_minutes_late help: The gap between the scheduled start and the moment the session was marked running. A habit of late starts shortens the class members paid for. sql: SELECT i.full_name AS instructor, ROUND(AVG(EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM (s.running_at - s.starts_at)) / 60)::numeric, 1) AS avg_minutes_late FROM bus_class_session s JOIN bus_instructor i ON i.id = s.instructor_id AND i.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.running_at IS NOT NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report sessions-empty-upcoming-by-class title: Upcoming sessions with nobody booked, by class entity: ClassSession chart: bar x: class y: empty_sessions help: Sessions that are open and empty. Each is a decision to cancel, promote or leave, and grouping by class shows which classes are repeatedly the empty ones. sql: SELECT o.name AS class, COUNT(*) AS empty_sessions FROM (SELECT s.id, s.starts_at, s.capacity, s.class_offering_id, s.room_id, s.instructor_id, s.status, COUNT(b.id) FILTER (WHERE b.status <> 'cancelled') AS taken FROM bus_class_session s LEFT JOIN bus_booking b ON b.class_session_id = s.id AND b.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE s.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY s.id) f JOIN bus_class_offering o ON o.id = f.class_offering_id AND o.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE f.status = 'scheduled' AND f.starts_at > now() AND f.taken = 0 GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report bookings-by-status title: Bookings by outcome entity: Booking chart: pie x: status y: bookings help: What happened to the places that were taken. Held is still to come; attended is revenue earned; cancelled, forfeited and no-show are the three ways a seat goes to waste. sql: SELECT status, COUNT(*) AS bookings FROM bus_booking WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report bookings-per-month title: Bookings made per month entity: Booking chart: line x: month y: bookings help: Demand in one line. Seasonal shape and trend both show here, and it is the figure every other report on this studio is eventually compared to. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', booked_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS bookings FROM bus_booking WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report bookings-lead-time title: How far ahead members book entity: Booking chart: bar x: lead_time y: bookings help: Days between booking and the class. Mostly same-day means the studio's marketing windows are wasted; mostly weeks ahead means members plan, and a cancellation policy matters more. sql: SELECT CASE WHEN s.starts_at - b.booked_at < interval '1 day' THEN 'same day' WHEN s.starts_at - b.booked_at < interval '3 days' THEN '1-3 days' WHEN s.starts_at - b.booked_at < interval '7 days' THEN '3-7 days' ELSE 'over a week' END AS lead_time, COUNT(*) AS bookings FROM bus_booking b JOIN bus_class_session s ON s.id = b.class_session_id AND s.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE b.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report bookings-late-cancellations title: Late cancellations by class entity: Booking chart: bar x: class y: late_cancellations help: Places cancelled inside the twelve-hour cutoff, by class. These are the cancellations the studio cannot refill, so the classes that attract them are the ones to look at. sql: SELECT o.name AS class, COUNT(*) AS late_cancellations FROM bus_booking b JOIN bus_class_session s ON s.id = b.class_session_id AND s.deleted_at IS NULL JOIN bus_class_offering o ON o.id = s.class_offering_id AND o.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE b.deleted_at IS NULL AND b.status IN ('cancelled', 'forfeited') AND b.cancelled_at IS NOT NULL AND b.cancelled_at > s.starts_at - interval '12 hours' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report bookings-no-show-rate-by-level title: No-show rate by class level entity: Booking chart: bar x: level y: no_show_pct help: Whether beginners or advanced students are the ones who fail to appear. The answer decides who gets the reminder text. sql: SELECT o.level, ROUND(100.0 * COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE b.status = 'no_show') / NULLIF(COUNT(*), 0), 1) AS no_show_pct FROM bus_booking b JOIN bus_class_session s ON s.id = b.class_session_id AND s.deleted_at IS NULL JOIN bus_class_offering o ON o.id = s.class_offering_id AND o.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE b.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report bookings-attendance-by-weekday title: Attendance rate by day of the week entity: Booking chart: bar x: weekday y: attended_pct help: Of the places held for a day, the share that were used. A weekday with a poor rate is a day members over-book and abandon. sql: SELECT to_char(s.starts_at, 'Dy') AS weekday, ROUND(100.0 * COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE b.status = 'attended') / NULLIF(COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE b.status <> 'cancelled'), 0), 1) AS attended_pct FROM bus_booking b JOIN bus_class_session s ON s.id = b.class_session_id AND s.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE b.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.starts_at < now() GROUP BY 1, EXTRACT(ISODOW FROM s.starts_at) ORDER BY EXTRACT(ISODOW FROM s.starts_at)
    %%report bookings-fee-waivers-per-month title: Cancellation fees waived per month entity: Booking chart: line x: month y: waivers help: Each waiver is a rule not applied. A rising line means the cutoff is being negotiated rather than enforced. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', booked_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS waivers FROM bus_booking WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND fee_waived GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report bookings-waiver-reasons title: The reasons given for waiving a fee entity: Booking chart: bar x: reason y: waivers help: Waiver reasons, counted and grouped. A few reasons repeating tells the studio what its policy fails to allow for. sql: SELECT LOWER(TRIM(waiver_reason)) AS reason, COUNT(*) AS waivers FROM bus_booking WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND fee_waived AND waiver_reason IS NOT NULL AND TRIM(waiver_reason) <> '' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 15
    %%report bookings-paid-with-a-pack title: Bookings paid from a pack and bookings not entity: Booking chart: pie x: paid_from y: bookings help: A booking with no pack behind it was paid some other way, or not at all. The share without one is the size of the studio's off-system revenue risk. sql: SELECT CASE WHEN class_pack_id IS NULL THEN 'no pack' ELSE 'pack' END AS paid_from, COUNT(*) AS bookings FROM bus_booking WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND status <> 'cancelled' GROUP BY 1
    %%report bookings-per-session title: How many places each session sells entity: Booking chart: bar x: places_taken y: sessions help: The distribution of class sizes actually run. A spike at one or two is a timetable that is too wide; a spike at capacity is a waiting list the studio has not noticed. sql: SELECT f.taken AS places_taken, COUNT(*) AS sessions FROM (SELECT s.id, s.starts_at, s.capacity, s.class_offering_id, s.room_id, s.instructor_id, s.status, COUNT(b.id) FILTER (WHERE b.status <> 'cancelled') AS taken FROM bus_class_session s LEFT JOIN bus_booking b ON b.class_session_id = s.id AND b.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE s.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY s.id) f WHERE f.status IN ('completed', 'running') GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report bookings-forfeited-by-class title: Forfeited places by class entity: Booking chart: bar x: class y: forfeited help: Places lost to the cancellation rule rather than used, by class. The classes at the top are where members are being caught by a rule they did not expect. sql: SELECT o.name AS class, COUNT(*) AS forfeited FROM bus_booking b JOIN bus_class_session s ON s.id = b.class_session_id AND s.deleted_at IS NULL JOIN bus_class_offering o ON o.id = s.class_offering_id AND o.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE b.deleted_at IS NULL AND b.status = 'forfeited' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report bookings-time-to-cancel title: How long members hold a place before cancelling it entity: Booking chart: bar x: held_for y: cancellations help: Hours between taking a place and giving it back. Quick cancellations are indecision and cost nothing; slow ones are life getting in the way. sql: SELECT CASE WHEN cancelled_at - booked_at < interval '1 hour' THEN 'under an hour' WHEN cancelled_at - booked_at < interval '1 day' THEN 'under a day' WHEN cancelled_at - booked_at < interval '7 days' THEN 'under a week' ELSE 'over a week' END AS held_for, COUNT(*) AS cancellations FROM bus_booking WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND status = 'cancelled' AND cancelled_at IS NOT NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report bookings-repeat-attendance title: Members who keep coming back to the same class entity: Booking chart: bar x: class y: regulars help: Members who attended the same class three or more times, by class. These are the studio's regulars, and the classes with the most are the ones whose cancellation would hurt. sql: SELECT o.name AS class, COUNT(*) AS regulars FROM (SELECT s.class_offering_id, b.member_id FROM bus_booking b JOIN bus_class_session s ON s.id = b.class_session_id AND s.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE b.deleted_at IS NULL AND b.status = 'attended' GROUP BY 1, 2 HAVING COUNT(*) >= 3) g JOIN bus_class_offering o ON o.id = g.class_offering_id AND o.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report waitlist-by-status title: Waiting list entries by outcome entity: WaitlistEntry chart: pie x: status y: entries help: Waiting, promoted or expired. A large expired slice is people the studio failed to place in time. sql: SELECT status, COUNT(*) AS entries FROM bus_waitlist_entry WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report waitlist-depth-upcoming title: Queue length for upcoming sessions entity: WaitlistEntry chart: bar x: class y: waiting help: How many people are waiting on each class that has not yet run. The long queues are the sessions to add. sql: SELECT o.name AS class, COUNT(*) AS waiting FROM bus_waitlist_entry w JOIN bus_class_session s ON s.id = w.class_session_id AND s.deleted_at IS NULL AND s.starts_at > now() JOIN bus_class_offering o ON o.id = s.class_offering_id AND o.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE w.deleted_at IS NULL AND w.status = 'waiting' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report waitlist-promotion-rate title: Share of waiting list entries that become a place entity: WaitlistEntry chart: bar x: class y: promoted_pct help: How often waiting pays off, per class. A low rate means members queue for classes that never free a seat, and the right response is another session, not more patience. sql: SELECT o.name AS class, ROUND(100.0 * COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE w.status = 'promoted') / NULLIF(COUNT(*), 0), 1) AS promoted_pct FROM bus_waitlist_entry w JOIN bus_class_session s ON s.id = w.class_session_id AND s.deleted_at IS NULL JOIN bus_class_offering o ON o.id = s.class_offering_id AND o.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE w.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report waitlist-joins-per-month title: People joining a waiting list, per month entity: WaitlistEntry chart: line x: month y: joined help: Unmet demand over time. Every entry is somebody who wanted a class that was full, and the line is the studio's best argument for expanding the timetable. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', joined_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS joined FROM bus_waitlist_entry WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report waitlist-expired-by-weekday title: Waiting list entries that expired, by day of the week entity: WaitlistEntry chart: bar x: weekday y: expired help: Entries that ran out of time without a place. The weekdays with the most are where the studio is losing the most demand it could have kept. sql: SELECT to_char(s.starts_at, 'Dy') AS weekday, COUNT(*) AS expired FROM bus_waitlist_entry w JOIN bus_class_session s ON s.id = w.class_session_id AND s.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE w.deleted_at IS NULL AND w.status = 'expired' GROUP BY 1, EXTRACT(ISODOW FROM s.starts_at) ORDER BY EXTRACT(ISODOW FROM s.starts_at)
    %%report waitlist-members-queuing-repeatedly title: Members who keep ending up on waiting lists entity: WaitlistEntry chart: bar x: lists_joined y: members help: Members on several lists at once are the studio's most eager customers, and the ones most likely to be annoyed by a full class. sql: SELECT n AS lists_joined, COUNT(*) AS members FROM (SELECT member_id, COUNT(*) AS n FROM bus_waitlist_entry WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY member_id) t GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report packs-by-status title: Class packs by status entity: ClassPack chart: pie x: status y: packs help: Active packs are revenue still to be earned; exhausted ones were used in full; expired ones are credits that vanished. The expired slice is the one the studio should want smaller. sql: SELECT status, COUNT(*) AS packs FROM bus_class_pack WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report packs-purchased-per-month title: Packs bought per month entity: ClassPack chart: line x: month y: packs help: Sales of the thing the studio actually sells. The line to watch around the start of a term and after a price change. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', purchased_on), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS packs FROM bus_class_pack WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report packs-size-mix title: Packs sold by number of credits entity: ClassPack chart: bar x: credits y: packs help: Which pack sizes members choose. A heavy small-pack tail means the large packs are priced out of reach or not understood. sql: SELECT credits_purchased AS credits, COUNT(*) AS packs FROM bus_class_pack WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report packs-unused-credit-share title: Share of credits never used, by pack status entity: ClassPack chart: bar x: status y: unused_pct help: Credits bought and not spent. On expired packs this is revenue the member lost and may resent; on active ones it is the studio's liability. sql: SELECT status, ROUND(100.0 * SUM(credits_remaining) / NULLIF(SUM(credits_purchased), 0), 1) AS unused_pct FROM bus_class_pack WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report packs-expiring-by-week title: Credits expiring over the next ten weeks entity: ClassPack chart: bar x: week y: credits_expiring help: Credits that will vanish, week by week. The shape tells the desk when to send the reminders that bring members back. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('week', expires_on), 'MM-DD') AS week, SUM(credits_remaining) AS credits_expiring FROM bus_class_pack WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND status = 'active' AND expires_on BETWEEN now()::date AND now()::date + 70 GROUP BY 1, date_trunc('week', expires_on) ORDER BY date_trunc('week', expires_on)
    %%report packs-credits-remaining-bands title: Active packs by how many credits are left entity: ClassPack chart: bar x: credits_left y: packs help: How far through their packs members are. Many nearly-empty packs is a renewal moment; many untouched ones is members who bought and did not start. sql: SELECT CASE WHEN credits_remaining = credits_purchased THEN 'untouched' WHEN credits_remaining <= 2 THEN '1-2 left' WHEN credits_remaining * 2 <= credits_purchased THEN 'under half left' ELSE 'over half left' END AS credits_left, COUNT(*) AS packs FROM bus_class_pack WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND status = 'active' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report packs-expired-with-credits-per-month title: Credits lost to expiry, per month entity: ClassPack chart: line x: month y: credits_lost help: The credits that expired unused, month by month. Revenue the studio kept and goodwill it spent, and a line that should fall once reminders go out. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', expires_on), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, SUM(credits_remaining) AS credits_lost FROM bus_class_pack WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND status = 'expired' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report packs-days-to-first-booking title: How long members wait before using a new pack entity: ClassPack chart: bar x: wait y: packs help: Days from purchase to the first booking against it. A long wait is a member who bought on impulse and may not come back. sql: SELECT CASE WHEN f.first_booked - p.purchased_on <= 3 THEN 'within 3 days' WHEN f.first_booked - p.purchased_on <= 14 THEN '4-14 days' ELSE 'over 2 weeks' END AS wait, COUNT(*) AS packs FROM bus_class_pack p JOIN (SELECT class_pack_id, MIN(booked_at)::date AS first_booked FROM bus_booking WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND class_pack_id IS NOT NULL GROUP BY 1) f ON f.class_pack_id = p.id WHERE p.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report packs-used-up-quickly title: Packs used up within a month entity: ClassPack chart: bar x: month y: packs help: Packs exhausted within thirty days of purchase, by purchase month. These members would have bought a bigger pack, and the studio should have offered one. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', p.purchased_on), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS packs FROM bus_class_pack p JOIN (SELECT class_pack_id, MAX(booked_at)::date AS last_booked FROM bus_booking WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND class_pack_id IS NOT NULL GROUP BY 1) l ON l.class_pack_id = p.id WHERE p.deleted_at IS NULL AND p.status = 'exhausted' AND l.last_booked - p.purchased_on <= 30 GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report payments-by-method title: Money taken by payment method entity: Payment chart: pie x: method y: revenue help: Card, cash or bank transfer, by value. The mix decides what the desk has to bank, reconcile and fear. sql: SELECT method, SUM(amount) AS revenue FROM bus_payment WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
    %%report payments-average-by-month title: Average payment per month entity: Payment chart: line x: month y: average help: Whether members are buying bigger packs or smaller ones over time. Rising means trading up; falling means a promotion is eating the margin. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', received_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, ROUND(AVG(amount), 2) AS average FROM bus_payment WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report payments-cash-share-by-month title: Share of money taken in cash, per month entity: Payment chart: line x: month y: cash_pct help: Cash is the method that needs counting, banking and trusting. A rising share is more work and more risk for the desk. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', received_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, ROUND(100.0 * SUM(amount) FILTER (WHERE method = 'cash') / NULLIF(SUM(amount), 0), 1) AS cash_pct FROM bus_payment WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report payments-price-per-credit title: What a credit actually sells for, by month entity: Payment chart: line x: month y: price_per_credit help: Total received divided by credits sold. Discounts, bundles and promotions all show up here as a falling line, long before they show up in the accounts. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', p.purchased_on), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, ROUND(SUM(y.paid) / NULLIF(SUM(p.credits_purchased), 0), 2) AS price_per_credit FROM bus_class_pack p JOIN (SELECT class_pack_id, SUM(amount) AS paid FROM bus_payment WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1) y ON y.class_pack_id = p.id WHERE p.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
    %%report payments-largest title: The ten largest payments entity: Payment chart: bar x: reference y: amount help: The biggest single sums taken. Worth reading for outliers, because a fat-fingered amount is the commonest data-entry error a desk makes. sql: SELECT COALESCE(reference, id::text) AS reference, amount FROM bus_payment WHERE deleted_at IS NULL ORDER BY amount DESC LIMIT 10


